er” rule is a myth outside of certain regions. In many places, electric heat pumps have already crossed the threshold where they’re cheaper to run than gas, even without subsidies. --- ### 2. Heat pumps change the comparison, and that’s what people usually mean When someone says “I switched to electric and my bill went down,” they almost never replaced a gas furnace with baseboard heaters. They installed a **heat pump**. As I mentioned, a heat pump with a seasonal average COP of 2.5 to 3.5 can make the effective cost of electricity per BTU competitive with or lower than gas, *even in areas where resistive heat would be a disaster*. A lot of the anecdotes you’re hearing are really “heat pump vs. old gas furnace” comparisons, often combined with better insulation or duct sealing done at the same time. --- ### 3. The hidden costs of being connected to gas A gas utility bill has two parts: the **cost of gas** (per therm) plus a **fixed monthly customer charge**, which can be $10–$30 just to have a meter. If your home is small, well‑insulated, or in a mild climate, your total annual gas usage might be so low that that fixed charge dominates. Then, even if electric resistance heat costs a bit more per BTU, ditching the gas connection entirely can save money overall. People who do the math often find that a full‑electric home with a heat pump and no gas bill beats a gas furnace plus all those fixed fees. --- ### 4. Additional multipliers: solar, time‑of‑use, and your own EV Now layer on the fact that you already own an electric car and maybe have a home charger. Many utilities offer **time‑of‑use rates** that make electricity dirt‑cheap at night—perfect for both car charging and charging a thermal battery (the house). If you also have rooftop solar, your marginal cost of electricity during sun‑heavy hours can be near zero, making electric heat effectively free during the day. In that scenario, gas can’t compete even if its raw fuel cost is lower, because you’ve already sunk the cost into solar panels and a heat pump. --- ### 5. The EV analogy holds when you match the “engine” type Think of it this way: - **Electric vs. gasoline car**: You replace a ~25% efficient engine with a ~90% efficient motor → huge cost drop, but still depends on whether your local electricity price is high or low (some northeastern states with expensive electricity and cheap gas can make EVs only marginally cheaper). - **Electric resistance vs. gas furnace**: Replace ~90% efficient furnace with 100% efficient resistance → minimal efficiency gain, so cost is almost entirely price per BTU, and you lose where electricity is expensive. - **Heat pump vs. gas furnace**: Replace ~90% furnace with a machine that’s effectively 250–400% efficient (COP 2.5–4) → big cost drop, just like the EV, but again modulated by local prices and climate.
