heat pump might have a COP of 4. In a bitter cold snap, it might drop to 1.5 or even 1 (requiring backup resistance). Gas doesn’t have that performance cliff. So whether a heat pump is cheaper *everywhere* depends on the local climate—and thus the average COP over the heating season—plus the local electric and gas prices. That’s why even with heat pumps the universal rule breaks. --- ### A simple way to think about it - **EVs**: replace a terrible engine (20% efficient) with a great motor (90%) → huge efficiency gain that swamps almost any local price variation. - **Electric resistance heat**: replace a good furnace (90%) with a good heater (100%) → negligible gain, so the local price of energy rules. - **Heat pump heat**: replaces a good furnace (90%) with a variable-efficiency device (200–400%, but weather-dependent) → the gain is big but *varies*, so it reverts to “depends on where you live.” You could almost draw a rule: *when the old fossil-fuel technology is already relatively efficient, switching to electricity doesn’t guarantee a cost win unless you also install a high-efficiency electric device (heat pump) and live in a climate and price region that lets it shine.* For cars, the gasoline engine is so laughably inefficient that even a mediocre electric motor wins everywhere. For heating, gas furnaces were never that bad to begin with, so even a good electric alternative only wins under the right conditions. That’s the real difference in a nutshell—and it’s why all those “my electric house is cheaper” stories you hear are almost always in places with low power rates or come from people who installed heat pumps, not baseboards.
