ou jack electricity to $0.30/kWh and drop EV efficiency to 2.2 mi/kWh (extreme), you get $0.136/mile, which is a few cents more expensive. But that scenario requires a perfect storm: a very cold location with very high electric rates and an unusually inefficient EV. For most EV owners, even in winter, the cost per mile remains lower. And crucially, the motor is still 90% efficient; you’re just consuming more energy. **Heat pump winter worst-case (cold snap, older heat pump):** - Gas furnace: 90% efficient, $1.20/therm → $1.33 per 100k BTU. - Heat pump: at 5°F, COP plummets to 1.5. Electricity at $0.15/kWh → 100k BTU / (1.5 × 3412) = 19.5 kWh → $2.93. Almost double the cost. That’s a clear loss **because the COP itself fell to a level where the multiplier can’t offset the high price of electricity**. A gas furnace never suffers that kind of efficiency collapse. --- ### Why this matters for the “universal win” claim - **EVs**: The 4x powertrain efficiency advantage is rock-solid. Winter energy consumption might increase by 20–40%, but that’s like scaling up the electric bill by a factor of 1.2–1.4. The underlying cost per unit of useful motion is still dominated by the massive efficiency gap. So you can safely say “EVs are cheaper to run” for virtually everyone, even in winter, barring truly exotic pricing. - **Heat pumps**: Their efficiency advantage *evaporates* in deep cold, removing the multiplier entirely when you need heat the most. That’s not just a 40% penalty; it can be a 200% penalty relative to gas. So the statement “electric heat is cheaper” must be paired with “provided you have a modern cold-climate heat pump and don’t live in a region with very cheap gas and extremely cold winters.” --- ### The new cold-climate heat pumps are closing this gap, though Modern units can maintain COP above 2.0 down to -15°F. In that case, the variable multiplier is less of a problem, and the EV-like logic starts to hold: even a 30% drop in efficiency still leaves a sufficient gap to win in most places. That’s why all-electric new homes are now viable in many colder regions—they’re using these newer units. But we’re not yet at the point where *every* heat pump in *every* cold snap beats gas; the technology is still improving and isn’t universally deployed. So your intuition that I was applying a double standard is fair, but the actual distinction is that the EV’s efficiency gain is fixed and enormous, so it weathers the cold penalty while staying ahead; a traditional heat pump’s efficiency gain can disappear, handing the win back to gas. That’s why “it depends” still applies to heating but not to driving.
